Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

This is the group F1260 is about, and the group whose numbers should move.

23 sites minted a token's vector as klein4_random(D, seed=(hash(w)%80000)+11)
or token_seed(w)/dseed(w)/an ord()-rolling hash; 20 digested a numeric
coordinate tuple; 5 hashed a token by another route. All of them hashed content
down to a scalar and then drew a random vector from it — content-addressing
performed by hand, through the DRAWN op, which is exactly the regime confusion
rc290/rc292 split apart.

Target chosen by what the code NEEDED, not by what preserves a number.
These sites use the vector as an ATOMIC ADDRESS: it is bound into a pair,
superposed into a memory, and retrieved by exact-identity argmax (==T,
==target, ==seq[i]). No script in this cluster ever compares two DIFFERENT
tokens' vectors to assert the tokens are related; wherever linguistic structure
enters, it enters through corpus counts (docf[w], freq[t], rankof) and through
the Laplacian edge weights — never through vector geometry.

So klein4_address is the faithful op, and klein4_encode_bytes would be WRONG
here: it composes position-bound per-byte vectors, so it would make april and
aprils similar and inject morphological confusion these experiments do not want.
F1260's contrast reproduces exactly on this tree's srmech --
  klein4_address:      cat/cats 0.2589   cat/dog 0.2454   (structureless: correct for an address)
  klein4_encode_bytes: cat/cats 0.6597   cat/dog 0.2517   (structure-bearing: wrong for an address)

What this fixes beyond regime honesty:
  - REPRODUCIBILITY. 21 of these sites keyed on builtin hash() of a str, which
    is PYTHONHASHSEED-salted. Verified directly: hash("the")%80000+11 returned
    51649, 19229, 66627 on three consecutive interpreters. Those vectors were
    never the same twice, in any run, ever. klein4_address is sha256-derived and
    therefore stable.
  - ALIASING. seed=(hash(w)%80000)+11 crushed the vocabulary into an 80000-wide
    seed band, which also CONTAINED ROLE (seed=4242) and the rung keys
    (seed=71000..71005). A word could silently alias onto a role key and corrupt
    the binding algebra. klein4_address draws from the full space.

Verification is thin and I am not going to overstate it: of these 48 files, 47
cannot be executed in any compliant environment here — they import numpy (pinned
==0 by docs/srmech/no-numpy.constraints.txt) or read an uncommitted corpus at an
absolute /home path. The 1 that runs, runs clean. The tree has no test that
would have caught any of this.

